  "cut_point": "We are given a **circular table with 60 chairs** and asked to find the **largest number of people** that can sit around the table such that **each person is only sitting next to exactly one other person**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Understand the condition\n\nEach person can only sit next to **one other person**. That means:\n\n- Each person is sitting between **two people**.",
  "reflection": {
    "verified_state": [
      "There are 60 chairs arranged in a circle.",
      "Each seated person must have exactly one adjacent seated person among their two neighboring chairs."
    ],
    "error_step": "- Each person is sitting between **two people**.",
    "error_reason": "This incorrectly assumes both neighboring chairs are occupied. Having exactly one adjacent person means the other neighboring chair must be empty, so a person is not sitting between two people.",
    "new_direction": "Model the seating as a circular binary string and require each block of occupied chairs to have length exactly 2, separated by at least one empty chair; then maximize the number of occupied chairs."
  }
